# kw-common — the shared ops-alerting configuration.
#
# ONE file, read by EVERY service in the deployment. That is the whole point: moving the inbox,
# rotating the SMTP password or changing the mail host is one edit here, not one edit per service.
#
# WHERE IT GOES:  <SHARED_ROOT>/configs/alerting.env
# WHO READS IT:   kw_common.alerting_env at boot, and kw_common.alerting on every DELIVERED
#                 notification (a de-duplicated repeat reads nothing) — so a password rotated
#                 here takes effect without restarting anything.
# HOW TO USE IT:  copy this file to that path, fill in the values, and keep the file readable
#                 only by the account the services run as. It holds an SMTP password.
#
# Full instructions, including per-operating-system commands to create the structure:
#   docs/alerting-setup.md
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALMOST EVERY VALUE BELOW IS A PLACEHOLDER. `SMTP_PORT=587` is the exception — it is a real,
# working default and you only change it if your provider says otherwise. Everything else must be
# replaced, and comes in two kinds whose difference matters:
#
#   * `CHANGE-ME`  — boot validation REFUSES it. A required key still carrying this stops the
#                    service starting, by name, so it cannot be forgotten. That is why the values
#                    you are most likely to skip are spelled this way.
#   * `example.com` — a reserved documentation domain, so it resolves nowhere and delivers
#                    nothing. It is shown for the settings whose SHAPE is the point (a full ntfy
#                    URL, an address), and boot validation CANNOT tell it from a real setting.
#                    Nothing will catch one you leave behind except this sentence.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === email — the durable record ===============================================================
# ntfy's cache is ephemeral, so email is what is still there next week. Both channels fire for
# every notification; a dead channel must not be able to silence an alert.

# Where operator alerts are delivered. One address, or several separated by commas.
EMAIL_TO=CHANGE-ME-to-your-ops-address

# The From header the recipient sees. Keep it a PLAIN mailbox unless you also set SMTP_USER
# below: a display-name form (`Alerts <box@host>`) is a valid header and is NOT a credential, so
# it cannot stand in for the auth identity, and boot validation refuses rather than pretend.
EMAIL_FROM=CHANGE-ME-to-your-sending-address

# Your mail provider's submission host and port. 587 is STARTTLS submission and is what this
# library expects; the connection is verified against the system trust store, so a host whose
# certificate does not validate is refused rather than silently downgraded.
SMTP_HOST=CHANGE-ME-to-your-providers-smtp-host
SMTP_PORT=587

# OPTIONAL — send email through an HTTP CONNECT proxy: `host:port` or `http://host:port`. Leave it
# unset to connect directly. Port 587 (STARTTLS) and 465 (implicit TLS) both work through it, and
# the certificate is still verified against SMTP_HOST, not the proxy. A service can set its own
# SMTP_PROXY environment variable, which replaces this line for that service; set but blank means
# a direct connection even when this line names a proxy.
# SMTP_PROXY=proxy.example.com:3128

# OPTIONAL — the AUTH IDENTITY. When this line is absent or blank it defaults to EMAIL_FROM, but
# ONLY when EMAIL_FROM is a plain `local@domain`; a display-name or non-ASCII From header is not a
# credential and will not be used as one. Set it explicitly when the two differ: a verified alias,
# or a relay whose user is literally `apikey`.
# SMTP_USER=alerts@example.com

# The password or app-password for that identity. If your provider offers app-specific passwords,
# use one: it can be revoked without touching the account.
SMTP_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME


# === ntfy — the phone push ====================================================================
# ⚠️ THE FULL URL, NEVER A BARE TOPIC NAME. A bare topic is rejected loudly, because `urllib`
# cannot post to it and the channel would be dead while looking configured. Keeping the whole URL
# here also means self-hosting later is an edit to this file rather than a code change.

# The topics services share, one per environment. DEPLOY_ENV on each container selects between
# them. A service on a shared topic gets `[<env>][<service>]` in front of its alert titles,
# because on a shared topic nothing else says who is talking.
# ⚠️ THESE TWO KEEP THEIR example.com SHAPE ON PURPOSE — the full-URL form is the thing most
# often got wrong, and showing it is worth more than a marker boot validation would catch. They
# deliver nowhere; replace the host AND the topic.
NTFY_URL_DEV=https://ntfy.example.com/your-dev-topic
NTFY_URL_PROD=https://ntfy.example.com/your-prod-topic

# OPTIONAL — a dedicated topic for ONE service, which wins over the shared topic above and turns
# the title prefix OFF, because the topic is already the identifier.
#
# The key is `NTFY_URL_` followed by the service name, upper-cased, with each RUN of characters
# that are not an ASCII letter or digit replaced by a SINGLE underscore, and any leading or
# trailing ones dropped. ASCII is the word that matters: an accented letter is NOT a letter here,
# so `café-poller` derives NTFY_URL_CAF_POLLER. There is exactly one spelling, and a service whose key is spelled any other way is
# never looked up — it lands back on the shared topic, with a title prefix, silently:
#
#     service "poller"        ->  NTFY_URL_POLLER
#     service "backup-agent"  ->  NTFY_URL_BACKUP_AGENT
#     service "report.mailer" ->  NTFY_URL_REPORT_MAILER
#     service "feed--poller"  ->  NTFY_URL_FEED_POLLER      (a RUN becomes ONE underscore)
#     service "poller-"       ->  NTFY_URL_POLLER           (the ends are dropped)
#
# Two more rules, so a surprise is a refusal rather than a silent fallback: a service may NOT be
# named after an environment (`dev` or `prod` would derive the SHARED topic's key), and a name
# with no ASCII letters or digits is refused outright rather than reduced to nothing.
#
# NTFY_URL_BACKUP_AGENT=https://ntfy.example.com/your-backup-topic
